{"File":"/Users/jps/Dropbox/simula agulhas/project_report.tex","LineNumber":611,"LineContent":"\\noindent\\textbf{(7) forc} --- First-Order Reversal Curve (FORC) suite. The system simulates a complete family of first-order reversal curves \\cite{pike1999, roberts2000, ruta2017}. Each cycle saturates the array at $+B_{\\max}$, ramps down to a reversal field $B_{r, k}$ (spaced evenly from $+B_{\\max}$ to the minimum reversal field \\code{--forc\\_Br\\_min}), and sweeps back to $+B_{\\max}$. The sweep is repeated for a total of \\code{--forc\\_n\\_curves} cycles. By default, timing is automatically calculated so that each cycle has a fixed duration, which yields a total time $t_{\\mathrm{sim}} = N_{\\mathrm{curves}} \\cdot (t_{\\mathrm{sat}} + t_{\\mathrm{ramp\\_down}} + t_{\\mathrm{ramp\\_up}})$. However, this causes the field sweep rate to vary from cycle to cycle depending on the reversal depth. If a target sweep rate is specified via \\code{--forc\\_rate} $R$ [T/s] (V71), the simulator dynamically scales the ramp-up (and ramp-down, unless overridden by an explicit \\code{--forc\\_t\\_ramp\\_down}) durations for each cycle $k$ as $t_{\\mathrm{ramp\\_up}, k} = (B_{\\max} - B_{r, k})/R$. This maintains a constant sweep rate $dB/dt \\equiv R$ across all loops, adjusting the total $t_{\\mathrm{sim}}$ dynamically to the sum of these cycle durations. The parameter \\code{--forc\\_sweep} (\\code{both}, \\code{up}, or \\code{down}; V69) controls which sweep branches (decreasing, increasing, or both) are logged and plotted. The entire suite of curves is logged to \\code{forc\\_curve.csv} containing the reversal field $B_r$ as a fifth column:"}
