\medskip\noindent\textbf{Demagnetization procedure (V74)} --- Before any field mode is executed, an optional pre-treatment phase can be enabled (\code{--demag}) with two different physical algorithms (Method A and Method B from \code{compass\_sim\_protocol\_notes.md}):
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Method A: Biaxial Rotational Demagnetization} (\code{--demag rotational} or \code{on}) --- The external field vector rotates continuously in the $xy$-plane while its peak magnitude decays linearly to zero:
\begin{align*}
B_x(t) &= B_{\max} \cdot \left(1.0 - \frac{t}{t_{\text{demag}}}\right) \cdot \cos(2\pi \cdot f_{\text{demag}} \cdot t) \\
B_y(t) &= B_{\max} \cdot \left(1.0 - \frac{t}{t_{\text{demag}}}\right) \cdot \sin(2\pi \cdot f_{\text{demag}} \cdot t)
\end{align*}
where $t_{\text{demag}} = \code{--demag\_cycles}/\code{--demag\_freq}$. This forces all needles through a complete angular sweep in the plane, erasing any directional bias and providing a truer isotropic randomized demagnetized state ($S \to 0$).
\item \textbf{Method B: Linear Sweep with Simulated Thermal Annealing} (\code{--demag anneal}) --- The external field decays linearly along the primary direction ($\phi$) without rotation, but we introduce a temporary random thermal white noise torque $\tau_i^{\text{thermal}}(t) = \sigma(t) \cdot \eta_i(t)$ acting on each needle. The noise standard deviation decays linearly to zero starting from an initial intensity scaled by the temperature parameter \code{--demag\_temp} ($T$) and the reference torque scale $\tau_{\text{ref}} = I \omega_0^2$:
$$\sigma(t) = T \cdot \tau_{\text{ref}} \cdot \left(1.0 - \frac{t}{t_{\text{demag}}}\right)$$
Ramping down this artificial thermal noise alongside the field allows the needle configurations to escape shallow local energy minima and settle into an isotropic global ground state.
\end{itemize}

